<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Puts the activity banner on a screen WITHOUT Revit, and measures it.

    The same source-linking approach Heron.StackGuard.TestHost already
    follows, for the same reason. HeronActivityBanner.cs touches no Autodesk
    type at all - it is pure WPF and two user32 calls - so it is linked here
    BY SOURCE rather than through a project reference. Referencing
    Heron.Revit.Addin would drag in RevitAPI.dll and make the one piece of
    add-in UI that can be tested anywhere untestable on any machine without
    Revit.

    That property is not an accident and it is worth defending: it turns an
    edit-and-see loop from "restart Revit" into about four seconds. Anything
    that puts an Autodesk reference into that file takes this whole project
    with it.

    TWO MODES, chosen by the first argument.

      shots   renders every state the banner has - reading, changing,
              finished, stopped, a long project name, and the case where no
              model name is known - to one PNG each, and prints the window
              handle beside each so six pictures can be shown to be six
              states of ONE window rather than six windows.

      freeze  the one that matters. Builds a stand-in main window on the main
              thread so the banner takes it as its OWNER exactly as it takes
              Revit's, blocks that thread for three seconds the way Execute
              blocks Revit's, and interrogates the banner FROM A THIRD THREAD
              while it is blocked. Prints whether the sweep moved and whether
              the elapsed time ticked.

    The freeze mode exists because the answer used to be no. On 2026-09-17,
    with the banner still living on Revit's thread, a DispatcherTimer on it
    fired zero times across that block and the sweep did not advance one
    pixel - so the card was a still picture for precisely the stretch it
    exists to cover, and nothing had caught it. See the 2026-09-17 note in
    docs/handover-archive.

    Building it also serves as the version gate. TargetFramework follows
    HeronTfm, so building it for each release is what proves the banner still
    compiles for every runtime Revit uses - net472 through net10.0-windows.
    A change that draws beautifully on .NET 10 and cannot build for Revit
    2020 is caught here and nowhere else.

        Run it with `dotnet run`, pointed at this folder, passing
        -p:RevitVersion=2024 and then the mode. (XML comments cannot carry a
        double hyphen, so the flags are spelled out rather than shown.)
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>$(HeronTfm)</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Heron.Banner.TestHost</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Heron.Banner.TestHost</AssemblyName>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <UseWPF Condition="'$(HeronTfm)' != 'net472' AND '$(HeronTfm)' != 'net48'">true</UseWPF>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\..\revit\Heron.Revit.Addin\HeronActivityBanner.cs"
             Link="HeronActivityBanner.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- .NET Framework does not get WPF from UseWPF; it is referenced. The
       add-in's own project file does the same, for the same releases. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(HeronTfm)' == 'net472' OR '$(HeronTfm)' == 'net48'">
    <Reference Include="PresentationCore" />
    <Reference Include="PresentationFramework" />
    <Reference Include="WindowsBase" />
    <Reference Include="System.Xaml" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
